id: decisions/032-which-layer-adapts
title: "Questions adapt, chrome and palette do not"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'presentation-adaptivity'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: presentation-adaptivity
axis_gloss: "whether the surface reshapes itself to the reader and context, or every reader gets the same canonical rendering"
poles:
  a: adaptive-presentation
  b: fixed-canonical-rendering
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: full
leverage: 8.4
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(7) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Which parts of a surface may change for the reader — the order, wording,
  pacing and medium of the questions — and which must look the same to everyone
  — the palette, the chrome, the meaning of a colour?

why_it_matters: >
  The authored rule says question presentation is noncanonical and may adapt
  under an explicit preference, and a test pins that tie-break; another test
  pins eight hex values and two pixel heights as canonical. Both pass, and no
  record says they govern different layers. Once answered, a bot rendering a
  View knows which fields it may reshape for an Agent or an accessibility need
  and which it may not touch, and a child Yawn's stated preference knows how far
  it can reach.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    3 judgments sit at adaptive-presentation (3 authored) and 4 at
    fixed-canonical-rendering (0 authored). The repository asserts both directions
    on this axis.
  attributed_to: TAXONOMY.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether accessibility needs (contrast, motion, font size) may override the
    pinned palette and chrome; core/orientation.yawn lists accessibility as an
    adaptation input, the brand test does not.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Every layer adapts. Palette, chrome geometry and question presentation
      all vary by Agent, Arena, accessibility need and accepted preference; the
      interface-contracts test's hex and pixel pins are removed and yawn-brand-v1
      becomes a default, not a canon.
    cost: "Removes the one canonical palette from interface/yawn-brand-v1.yawn and the assertions in tests/interface-contracts.test.mjs; semantic colour roles (observation, question, agent) lose their fixed meaning."
    risk: "Two readers of the same coordinate see different colours for the same role and the repo can no longer say what a View looks like."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Delete the palette assertion from tests/interface-contracts.test.mjs and add a preference field for palette to schemas/projection-preference.v1.schema.json."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0097"
      - "J0827"
      - "J1024"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: One canonical rendering for everyone. Questions render in
      canonical_question_order with a fixed exact_rendered_prompt, one palette, one
      chrome; accepted_question_order_preference and explicit_current_turn_choice
      are removed from the tie-break.
    cost: "Rewrites core/orientation.yawn orientation_inquiry.rule and core/inquiry-selection.yawn determinism.tie_break; breaks tests/orientation-map-v0.1.test.mjs which asserts the preference-aware ordering."
    risk: "Accessibility need is no longer an adaptation input, which core/orientation.yawn names explicitly."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Change tests/orientation-map-v0.1.test.mjs to assert canonical_question_order is the only tie-break; today it asserts the opposite and passes."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0480"
      - "J0490"
      - "J0868"
      - "J1232"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Three layers, named once in interface/node.yawn: the semantic layer is
      fixed (question_key set, semanticColorRoles, coordinate); the chrome layer is
      fixed and test-pinned (yawn-brand-v1 palette, yawn-chrome-v1 heights,
      prohibited banners and motifs); the presentation layer adapts (question order,
      wording, pacing, representation_medium) but only under an explicit accepted
      preference or accessibility need, with the question_key unchanged and a hashed
      selection receipt. Every interface contract declares which layer it governs.
    cost: "One paragraph in interface/node.yawn and a layer: line on each of the fifteen interface contracts."
    risk: "Accessibility contrast needs collide with the pinned palette and no layer owns the conflict."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Keep tests/interface-contracts.test.mjs (palette, chrome) and tests/orientation-map-v0.1.test.mjs (tie-break) as they are; add one assertion that interface/node.yawn declares presentation_layers with fixed and adaptive lists and that every interface/*.yawn carries a layer: value from that list."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0097"
      - "J0827"
      - "J1024"
      - "J0868"
      - "J1232"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.8
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    This is the rare axis where both sides carry a proof-attached judgment and
    both tests pass today, which means the tree already lives in C and only the
    sentence naming the layers is missing. The adaptive side is authored (three
    judgments) and scoped to question presentation; the fixed side is revealed and
    scoped to chrome and colour roles. Writing the layers down costs a paragraph
    and changes no test; the one genuine open point is where accessibility
    overrides sit.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "interface/node.yawn names the fixed and adaptive layers and every interface/*.yawn carries a layer: line."
    - "tests/interface-contracts.test.mjs and tests/orientation-map-v0.1.test.mjs both still pass unchanged."
    - "A new per-reader palette or chrome variation fails a test."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    An interface contract varies palette or chrome per reader while
    interface/yawn-brand-v1.yawn still claims one canonical palette and the suite
    stays green.
  status: proposed

evidence_sample:
  - "J0097"
  - "J0827"
  - "J1024"
  - "J0480"
  - "J0490"
  - "J0868"
  - "J1232"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
  id_index: TAXONOMY.md
